diagnosis_text:
I investigated the IT lab machine it_lab3 and the LAN infrastructure it connects to. The network interface on it_lab3 (eth0) is up with a valid IP (192.168.1.5/24) and default route via 192.168.1.1. The link is physically healthy (ethtool shows Link detected: yes, Full duplex). From it_lab3 I can reach the local peer it_lab2 (192.168.1.4) and the default gateway (192.168.1.1) with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs. From it_lab3 I can also reach remote subnets (e.g., office_pc 192.168.3.2, pc0 192.168.4.2, Internet VLAN host 128.168.0.3) successfully. In the reverse direction, router0 and it_lab2 can ping it_lab3 reliably. TX/RX counters on it_lab3 increment appropriately during these tests.

Mechanistically, if the reported “dead port” or isolation were present, we would see link down, no ARP resolution, or inability to reach the gateway; none of these symptoms are observed. Localization tests across L2 (lan_it) and L3 paths confirm proper switching via switch0 and routing via router0.

Root cause: No current network-side fault exists; it_lab3 is online and reachable across the campus network.

Corrective fix: No corrective network changes are required. The machine is back on the network now. If the user still experiences issues, focus on end-host factors (application/DNS/proxy/browser) and capture timestamps for any recurrence so we can correlate with logs; but the switch port, NIC, and routing are functioning normally. 
=== END ===